#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079656 :tutorial/content "When writing out a sum, we often have to write a lot of terms. For example, the following sum has five terms:\n\n$$\nS = 2+4+6+8+10\n$$\n\nWe could just as easily encounter a sum with ten terms, or a hundred terms, or a thousand terms, or even more!\n\nThankfully, there is a notation called **sigma notation** that allows us to write sums more compactly, without writing every term.\n\nTo express the given sum $S$ using sigma notation, we first need to come up with the general term $a_n$ in our sum. Here, the $n$th term of our sum has the form $a_n = 2n$, where $n$ ranges from ${\\color{red}1}$ to ${\\color{blue}5}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{1} &= 2(1) = 2 \\\\\na_{2} &= 2(2) = 4 \\\\\na_{3} &= 2(3) = 6 \\\\\na_{4} &= 2(4) = 8 \\\\\na_{5} &= 2(5) = 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our sum can be written as\n\n$$\nS = a_{\\color{red}1} + a_2 + a_3 + a_4 + a_{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the Greek letter $\\displaystyle\\sum$ (called \"sigma\") to denote our summation:\n\n$$\nS = ∑_(n = 1)^(5)(a_{n}) = ∑_(n = 1)^(5)(2n)\n$$\n\nWe write the $n$th term\n\n$$\n({\\color{black}a_n=2n})\n$$\n\nto the right of $\\displaystyle\\sum$, we put the first index $({\\color{red}1})$ below the sigma as\n\n$$\nn={\\color{red}1}\n$$\n\nand we put the last index $({\\color{blue}5})$ above the sigma.\n\nWe can retrieve the expanded sum by substituting all values between $\\color{red}1$ and $\\color{blue}5$ into the $n$th term, and separating each result with a \"$+$\" sign, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 1)^(5)(2n) &= 2(1) + 2(2) + 2(3) + 2(4) + 2(5) \\\\\n&= 2 + 4 + 6 + 8 + 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The sum\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{5} 2n = 2+4+6+8+10\n$$\n\nis an example of a **series**. A series is what we get when we add the terms of a *sequence* together. In this case, we're summing the terms of the sequence $a_n = 2n$." :tutorial/id #uuid "27542f45-7f00-500f-8ef4-8f36f6f8b957" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2837 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079610 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to express the following series in sigma notation.\n\n$$\nS = \\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}2} + \\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}3} + \\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}4} + \\cdots + \\dfrac{\\color{red}19}{\\color{blue}20} + \\dfrac{\\color{red}20}{\\color{blue}21}\n$$\n\nTo do this, we first examine the numerators and denominators of the corresponding sequence.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}2}, \\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}3}, \\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}4}, \\quad \\ldots, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\color{red}19}{\\color{blue}20}, \\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}20}{\\color{blue}21}\n$$\n\nOur goal is to find a pattern in the terms. To do this, we note the following:\n\n- The numerators form the following sequence:\n${\\color{red}1}, \\quad {\\color{red}2}, \\quad {\\color{red}3}, \\quad \\dots, \\quad {\\color{red}19}, \\quad {\\color{red}20}$\nNotice that we have $20$ terms in total. The $n$th term of this sequence is given by\n${\\color{red}a_n} = {\\color{red}n}, \\qquad 1\\leq n \\leq 20$.\n- The denominators form the following sequence:\n${\\color{blue}2}, \\quad {\\color{blue}3}, \\quad \\dots, \\quad {\\color{blue}20}, \\quad {\\color{blue}21}$\nSince the value in the denominator is always *one more than* the numerator, the $n$th term of this sequence is given by\n${\\color{blue}b_n} = {\\color{blue}n + 1}, \\qquad 1\\leq n \\leq 20$.\n\nTherefore, the $n$th term of the sequence\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}2}, \\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}3}, \\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}4}, \\quad \\ldots, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\color{red}19}{\\color{blue}20}, \\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}20}{\\color{blue}21}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}a_n}{\\color{blue}b_n} =\\dfrac{\\color{red}n}{\\color{blue}n+1}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ ranges from $n=1$ to $n=20$.\n\nTherefore, we can express our series using sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\nS = ∑_(n = 1)^(20)\\frac{n}{n + 1}\n$$\n\nWe can check that this answer is correct by writing out the summation in full and checking that we recover the original sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 1)^(20)\\frac{n}{n + 1} &= \\frac{1}{1 + 1} + \\frac{2}{2 + 1} + ⋯ + \\frac{19}{19 + 1} + \\frac{20}{20 + 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{2}{3} + ⋯ + \\frac{19}{20} + \\frac{20}{21}✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0c45a9c6-5ee9-5a89-8398-6a3eb37f5f63" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13578 :tutorial/title "Expressing Sums in Sigma Notation"}]}
